    "plusminus": {
      "type": "binder-prose",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "Plus, minus and listing notes",
        "anchor": "plus-minus",
        "layout": "stack",
        "style": "page",
        "text": "<p>Some listings add a plus or minus after the condition, like <strong>LP+</strong> or <strong>NM−</strong>. Think of it as a finer note inside the same condition: LP+ sits at the cleaner end of Lightly Played, NM− at the more worn end of Near Mint. TCGplayer's standard doesn't use plus or minus. It's a shorthand used on this shop's listings.</p>"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "notes": {
          "type": "panel",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Listing notes",
            "text": "<p>A card page can also show details called out about that exact copy under <strong>Noted in listing</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Holo swirl</strong> and <strong>holo bleed</strong> describe how the holofoil was printed on that copy. They come from the factory, not from wear, and some collectors look for them.</li><li><strong>Creased</strong> means the listing points out a crease.</li><li><strong>Clean front</strong> means the listing points out that the front looks clean even though the card has a flaw somewhere else.</li></ul>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "notes"
      ]
    },
    "graded": {
      "type": "binder-prose",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "Raw cards vs graded cards",
        "anchor": "raw-vs-graded",
        "layout": "columns",
        "style": "raised",
        "text": "<p>Conditions and grades answer the same question in different ways. A condition is a range. A grade is a single number from a grading company.</p>"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "raw": {
          "type": "panel",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Raw card",
            "text": "<p>A raw card is out of any grading case, usually in a sleeve. It is listed with a condition (NM, LP, MP, HP or DMG) that describes how much wear to expect.</p><p>You can hold it, play with it, put it in your binder, or send it to be graded yourself later.</p>"
          }
        },
        "slab": {
          "type": "panel",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Graded card",
            "text": "<p>A graded card has been sent to a professional grading company such as PSA, examined, given a number grade (for example PSA 10 Gem Mint, PSA 9 Mint, PSA 8 NM-MT) and sealed in a labeled plastic case called a slab.</p><p>On graded listings here, the grade shown is the one printed on the grading company's label.</p>"
          }
        },
        "why": {
          "type": "panel",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why they don't line up exactly",
            "text": "<p>Grading companies look at centering, corners, edges and surface on a finer scale. Two Near Mint raw cards can come back with different grades, so a condition tells you the range of wear, not a future grade.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "raw",
        "slab",
        "why"
      ]
    },
    "check": {
      "type": "binder-prose",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "Check a card yourself",
        "anchor": "check-a-card",
        "layout": "steps",
        "style": "page",
        "text": "<p>Whether you're looking at listing photos or a card in your hand, these checks catch most of what decides a condition.</p>"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "s1": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Start with the back",
            "text": "<p>The dark blue back of a Pokémon card is the easiest place to spot <strong>whitening</strong>: tiny white chips along the edges and corners where the blue has worn away. On the front, a yellow or silver border hides the same wear.</p>"
          }
        },
        "s2": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Tilt the holo toward a light",
            "text": "<p>Scratches in holofoil often vanish when you look straight on, then show up as thin bright lines when the light catches them. A cluster of fine scratches looks like a cloudy patch, which collectors call <strong>scuffing</strong>.</p>"
          }
        },
        "s3": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Look along the card from the side",
            "text": "<p>Hold the card at eye level. A <strong>bend</strong> shows as a gentle curve. A <strong>crease</strong> is a sharp line that catches the light, sometimes with a white ridge on the back.</p>"
          }
        },
        "s4": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Check each corner",
            "text": "<p>A soft, slightly rounded or frayed corner is wear. A corner that's lifted, split or missing a piece is damage.</p>"
          }
        },
        "s5": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Separate printing from wear",
            "text": "<p>Off-center borders, print lines, holo swirl and holo bleed come from the factory. Under TCGplayer's standard, printing quirks are judged against what's normal for that print run, and centering up to 70/30 doesn't lower a card's condition.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "s1",
        "s2",
        "s3",
        "s4",
        "s5"
      ]
    },
